contentSections.body:
```typescript
async function getUser(id: string): Promise<User> {
  const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
  const data = await response.json();
  return data as User;
}
```

`data as User` is a TypeScript type assertion — it tells the compiler "trust me, this is a `User`," and the compiler believes it, at compile time, with zero runtime consequence. `response.json()` returns `any`; TypeScript has genuinely no way to know what shape the actual response body has. If the API returns `{"error": "not found"}` instead of a user object — a completely normal thing for an API to do — `data as User` happily lies about it, and every line of code downstream that trusts `user.email` being a string will find out the hard way, at runtime, with a type system that promised this could never happen.

