function publicProfile(user) {
  return _.pick(user, ['id', 'displayName', 'avatarUrl']);
}
```

Plus a new line in `package.json`, plus lodash's own dependency tree, for a task that's three lines of plain JavaScript:

```javascript
function publicProfile(user) {
  const { id, displayName, avatarUrl } = user;
  return { id, displayName, avatarUrl };
}
```

Neither version is wrong. But one of them added a new external package — with its own install size, its own update cadence, its own transitive dependencies — to solve a problem the language's own destructuring syntax already solves in three lines.

